<description>
Recenter each four-pixel color mask in a shared compact canvas.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- Every non-background color appears as exactly four pixels.
- Each color's four-pixel mask has an odd-compatible row and column center
  when compared with the widest/tallest foreground mask.

Rule:
- For each non-background color, take its four pixels relative to that color's bbox.
- Make the output background-colored with size equal to the maximum foreground
  bbox height and width.
- Paste every relative color mask centered in that common output canvas.

Key insight:
- The large input only separates the colored masks spatially; the desired image
  is their center-aligned overlay after removing the surrounding background.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "four-pixel foreground color masks on a dominant background",
  "transform": "crop each color mask to its bbox and overlay the masks centered on a shared background-colored canvas",
  "grid_shape": "max foreground mask bbox height x max foreground mask bbox width"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    input: 10-18 rows x 8-19 cols, large enough to scatter all masks without pixel collisions
    output: S x S square where S = max bbox dim across all colors, S in {3, 5, 7} (always odd)
  background: single dominant color; occupies ~88-95% of input
  foreground_colors:
    count: 2-4 distinct non-background colors
    pixels_per_color: exactly 4
  masks:
    pattern: each 4-pixel mask is either corners (4 corners of its bbox) or diamond (4 edge midpoints of its bbox)
    exactly_one_diamond: exactly 1 color uses diamond pattern; all others use corners [observed]
    bbox_dims: each dimension is odd, in range 3-S; at least one mask has bbox height = S and at least one has bbox width = S
    centering_parity: (S - bbox_h) and (S - bbox_w) are both even (automatic since all dims odd)
    non_square_bbox: always uses corners pattern [observed]
  non_overlap: when all masks are centered in the S x S output canvas, no two colored pixels coincide
  transform: crop each color's 4 pixels to its bbox, center-align all bboxes on a shared S x S background canvas

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick S from {3, 5, 7}. Pick background color and 2-4 distinct foreground colors.
    2. For each foreground color, choose odd bbox dims (h, w) where 3 <= h <= S and 3 <= w <= S, with (S-h) and (S-w) even. Ensure max(all h) = S and max(all w) = S.
    3. Assign exactly one color the diamond pattern; assign corners to all others.
    4. Place all 4-pixel masks centered in the S x S canvas. Verify no two pixels overlap; if collision, retry assignment.
    5. To build the input, pick a grid large enough (roughly 2-3x S in each dim). For each color, choose a random top-left offset for its bbox such that it fits in the grid and no foreground pixel collides with another color's pixels. Place the 4 pixels at their bbox-relative positions shifted by the offset.
```
